00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:26.020
MICHEL DEGRAFF: I think it's a nice topic
to end this semester, because in a way, it

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:32.340
brings together, I think, some of the main
threads that we've through this semester.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:38.980
But also, in fact, the last point of the text,
alliteration I can sense my point to wait

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.360
into the future.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:45.460
So without further ado, we have Lorraine,
Will, and Cynthia.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:51.870
GUEST SPEAKER: So this is our presentation
on language resistance and liberation.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:53.789
Welcome to the last day of class.

00:00:53.789 --> 00:00:57.760
Congratulations (CHUCKLING) for getting this
far.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.120
So as the road map, we're going to go through
first "Rethinking Literacy, A Dialogue," the

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:04.269
piece by Freire and Macedo.

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:05.269
Macedo?

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:06.500
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Freire and Macedo.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:07.000
GUEST SPEAKER: Macedo?

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:07.500
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Macedo.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.360
GUEST SPEAKER: Macedo.

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:13.040
And then we're going to look at the "Literacy
and Critical Pedagogy" piece, and then end

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.299
with the MIT Haiti initiatives.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:19.860
GUEST SPEAKER: OK.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.400
So this is just a little bit of a background
on the writers of the first two pieces.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.210
Both of them are educators in critical pedagogy.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.320
And one is from Brazil, and one is Cape Verdean-American.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:42.040
GUEST SPEAKER: So in the first piece, Freire
mentions the premise-- the main premises of

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.501
culture, and I think that these are really
important to keep in mind, because this is

00:01:44.501 --> 00:01:49.520
a really interesting and, I think, powerful
way to frame culture.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:55.500
The first one is that cultural processes are
intimately connected with social relations,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.390
especially with class relations and class
formations, with sexual divisions, with racial

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:04.200
structuring of social relations, and with
age oppressions as a form of dependency.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.980
Got it? [chuckling]

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:13.080
OK. The second one, culture involves power and
helps to produce asymmetries in the abilities

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.110
of individuals and social groups to define
and realize their needs.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:22.760
And third one, culture is neither autonomous
nor an externally determined field, but a

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.520
site of social difficulties and struggles.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.400
Those ideas are really important to keep in
mind, because this paper also raises the idea--

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.250
I know it is a social field, which is kind
of the combination of all of the beliefs,

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:48.530
myths, aspirations, hopes of a part of a society,
of a culture, and so it includes things such

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:55.890
as why racial hierarchies exist, to why sexual
divisions exist, and like what roles gender

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.700
people play in a society.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.940
And it's really important to keep that in
mind, because if you're able to keep that

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:04.380
in mind, then you're able to think critically
about that.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.640
And through the critical process of engaging
with it, you're able to change the social field.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.240
And so I think that that's important, because
even though this paper raises a lot of really

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.510
powerful ideas, it's still not perfect.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:20.990
So one of the quotes of this paper is, "Literacy's
oral dimension is important, even if it takes

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:26.130
place in a culture, like that of the United
States, whose memory is predominantly written,

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:30.939
not oral, like that of Africa."

00:03:30.939 --> 00:03:36.269
Africa is not (LAUGHING) country.

00:03:36.269 --> 00:03:41.660
And so something like that is also part of the
social field, and would make it into this paper.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:47.240
Because even though Freire and Macedo are
thinking critically about this, they are not

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:53.940
engaging with the full reality of cultural
objectivity and being subjects within that.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.120
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:04.800
So in the next article, Paulo Freire talks
about the use of language as a form of resistance.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.079
And he first starts talking about how the
current traditional methods in literacy how

00:04:10.079 --> 00:04:16.280
they tend to ignore the importance of language
in a person's education.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:21.220
And he bases a lot of his theories on his
experiences.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:28.010
And from the second quote he says that, "The
sad reality is that while education in Portuguese

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:32.410
provides access to positions of political
and economic power for the higher echelon

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:37.540
of African society, it screens out the majority
of the masses, who fail to learn Portuguese

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:42.380
well enough to acquire the necessary literacy
skills for social, economic, and political

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:43.580
advancement."

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:48.780
And you definitely-- from the way that he
mentions this, you could definitely see a

00:04:48.791 --> 00:04:53.450
lot of like correlations between what's going
on around the world and currently in the United

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.820
States.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:07.220
And you could definitely see that the 1% usually
have the most control over things, and that

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:10.430
they usually tends to drive how policies are
made.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:15.080
But the fact is that when a person is taught
in their native language, they tend to learn

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.280
things better and comprehend things faster.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:21.600
And can we go to the next slide?

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:27.760
There's usually backlash when it comes to
different languages taught in schools.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.600
For example, in the US, people say that students
should learn how to speak standard American

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:34.900
English.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:41.670
But when it comes to providing these sort
of opportunities to minority groups of, I

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:46.790
guess for instance, like students of color,
there is usually some racial tensions that

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:49.960
develop that make minority students feel inferior.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:56.140
For example, there was an article mentioned
of like recently, like March of 2017, that

00:05:56.150 --> 00:06:04.630
talked about a robotics team that recently
had won the challenge, and the fact that after

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:09.310
they had won the students-- or I guess people
in the crowd started screaming at them and

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:12.000
telling them to go back to Mexico.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.690
And so this form of like demeaning commentary
tends to drive the dropout rates of minority

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:24.090
groups, and all of this tends to contribute
to like the prison-- sorry, the school to

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:25.220
prison pipeline.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:30.650
As you can see in the infographic, it gives
a lot of percentages talking about this sort

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:31.650
of issue.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:39.590
You can see in the bottom, it says that 68%
of all males in state and federal prison they

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:41.030
do not have a high school diploma.

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:47.330
And this tends to be-- gives statistics on
students.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:53.340
And all of this tends to stem from the fact
that minority students do not have the same

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:56.600
access to education as the elite part of society.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:00.520
And from personal experience, I can definitely
relate to this.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:06.100
I went to a school about like an hour and
a half from my house, and I definitely did

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:08.680
have a lot of good role models growing up.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:15.130
But were definitely students around my neighborhood
that didn't have the same access to the same

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:18.180
opportunities that I did.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.340
Some of them did drop out to start working.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:23.100
Others were influenced by gangs around my
neighborhood.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:27.960
And you could definitely-- things are definitely
getting better, but things could definitely

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:37.780
be improved from an emancipatory literacy
approach, which is what Paulo Freire mentions.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:38.640
Yeah.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:45.220
So in emancipatory literacy, this involves
using the language of the people, rather than

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:46.220
the elites.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:49.480
It teaches students to accept their experiences
and their culture.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:55.080
And this moves from the world of objectivity
to the world of subjectivity, where individuals

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:59.240
learn to accept themselves for who they are,
rather than feel like they're a victim of

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:00.810
society.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:07.440
And Paulo Freire does mention that achieving
emancipatory literacy is hard, but it's not

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:08.790
impossible.

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:14.700
And now I'll-- and so Lorraine wants to talk
about a few examples of how this is being

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:17.920
used today.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:24.300
So considering specifically the case of Haiti--
which Michel works a lot on, the MIT Haiti

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:29.860
initiative-- virtually all of the schooling
in both K through 12 education and then in

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:33.950
higher education is done in French, and not
in Kreyol.

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:39.370
But the problem is that most people only speak
Kreyol fluently, so like 95% or so, and very

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:42.370
few people speak French fluently, at all.

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:46.250
So when you go through school, and everything
is in a language that you don't understand,

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:49.850
how are you supposed to be able to actually
get any of the education, get any of this

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:54.450
social engagement, especially if children
are punished for speaking Kreyol in school.

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:59.410
And they can't interact with each other, can't
ask questions, can't gather any of the information

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:01.530
that's coming at them.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:07.550
So it's based on this false belief and really
outdated belief about the hierarchy of languages,

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:12.420
about which languages are created or are younger,
or anything like that, and that Kreyol isn't

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:18.800
developed enough to be able to be used for
technical language for STEM education to have

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:22.020
anything translated into Kreyol.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:26.420
And part of that is saying that like Kreyol
isn't widespread enough, even though Kreyol

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.580
has almost 10 million speakers.

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:33.450
Finnish has only about 5.5 million speakers,
and yet in Finland all of that education is

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:34.990
done in Finnish.

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:41.700
And people have-- studies have found that,
in Finland, kids are much better equipped

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:47.140
to be able to learn a second language, and
to go into higher education and gather all

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:53.690
of these like technical studies, because all
of their education is done in Finnish.

00:09:53.690 --> 00:10:02.840
And over at IAP, I was doing MISTI GTL in
the Catalonia region, in Spain, and there

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:06.810
Catalan also has a little under 10 million
speakers.

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:11.970
But in Catalonia all of the education is in
Catalan, which made it difficult for me as

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:16.860
like a "Spanish speaker"-- like not even fluent,
but trying to speak Spanish speaker-- to be

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:18.500
able to teach the students.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:26.030
But they had such a better experience with
all of their textbooks in Catalan all of their

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:27.030
computer software.

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:30.230
So I was trying to teach them Inkscape and
Gimp, but everything was is (CHUCKLING) Catalan,

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:34.100
and I just couldn't understand like even how
to open a window.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:36.330
So they taught me a lot about this.

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:41.660
And they-- all of them speak Catalan, and
Spanish, and English, and then they can like

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:44.470
learn German or French on the side, as well.

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:49.440
So all of them, because their initial education
K through 12 and then higher education, if

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:54.220
they want it to be, is in Catalan, then they
can learn all of these other things just as

00:10:54.220 --> 00:11:01.550
well as we do like in English, which is mostly
our one and kind of only language in the United

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:03.540
States.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:04.380
Yeah.

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:06.100
So that was my experience.

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:12.700
And what-- a school that is like a really
good model in Haiti is the LKM School.

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:15.440
I didn't know how to pronounce it in Kreyol,
so it's LKM.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:20.950
It's a K through 10 school in a rural part
on an island of Haiti, and their education

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:25.970
is starting to be in this model of all Kreyol
education, trying to teach all of these subjects,

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:27.580
all of the technical terms in Kreyol.

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:32.790
And the students are learning much better
than the rest of the students in Haiti, who's

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:38.380
schooling is French and who only 10% graduate
from high school.

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:42.840
So the linguistic apartheid part of this is
that, when you teach somebody in a language

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:47.660
they don't understand, you prevent them from
being able to move into different socioeconomic

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:54.190
classes to attain the education and the success
that they should be able to.

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:59.611
And this all leads to the MIT Haiti initiative
that Michel started here between MIT and all

00:11:59.611 --> 00:12:02.070
the Haitian education systems.

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:08.980
So a couple of their main points are to teach
educators in Haiti that teaching in Kreyol

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:13.650
is perfectly fine, that it is actually better
for the students, that it gives them more

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:18.790
opportunities, and that STEM and technical
subjects can be taught in Kreyol.

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:24.860
So a lot of the worries or concerns are that
like Kreyol doesn't have words for these languages.

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:28.550
We didn't have words for a computer either
up until when they were created.

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:32.180
A lot of other languages borrow words from
other languages.

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:37.450
So I learned Japanese, and in Japanese there's
this lovely thing called Katakana

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:40.510
where you just grab words from other languages.

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:44.440
So if you don't know how to pronounce it,
you kind of like make it sound like it, and

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:46.820
oftentimes, it is what has been borrowed.

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:49.940
So for example, bread is pan, from German.

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:54.130
And all of these other languages have words
borrowed from other languages, and that's

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:55.540
what globalization does.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:59.630
So the same thing should be able to happen
with Kreyol and STEM learning.

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:04.440
And the really good results of this are that
they affirm students' cultures and backgrounds,

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:09.400
and their own upbringings, and how the society
actually works, as opposed to a colonial oppressive

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:10.400
society.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:15.210
And it equalizes students being able to have
more social mobility, and to attain what they

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:18.360
want and what they should be able to.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:25.980
GUEST SPEAKER: So to kind of tie this back
to what we can do to stay woke, with regards

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:30.660
to literacy, I think it's important to remember
that, "language is a mediating force of knowledge,

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:32.720
but it is also knowledge itself."

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:39.760
And so one approach is for groups to kind
of come together and create a language that

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:43.900
describes their experience amongst themselves,
and then to share that language and to share

00:13:43.900 --> 00:13:47.360
that experience in their own language.

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:55.360
And I think that that is one way that we can
start breaking away from the kind of putting

00:13:55.360 --> 00:14:01.930
standard English on a pedestal, and can also
kind of validate other discourses, and other

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:06.940
ways of being, and of other just languages
that kind of already exist in the US and across

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:09.420
the world.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:16.040
Questions?

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:21.880
How have our understandings of literacy evolved
in response to the internet, unprecedented

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:25.950
global integration, and increased pressure
to preserve human rights?

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:30.120
For example, what does it mean that schools
repress the development of subjectivity, but

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.230
do largely ensure literacy?

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:37.010
How can modern notions of literacy evolve
to address the phenomena, such as the proliferation

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:38.560
of fake news?

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:44.980
So if we're moving past literacy as just the
idea of being able to read, and we are entering

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:50.910
this new kind of this new wave being defiantly
pushed by the internet and the influence of

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:56.390
technology on the way we live, literacy is
coming to be a more critical approach to life

00:14:56.390 --> 00:15:01.620
in the sense that fake news is a thing, you
know, and it's actually quite hard to distinguish

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:03.180
what news is really what news is fake.

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:08.850
And the ability to do that, having that critical
lens, is maybe the next evolution of literacy.

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:12.760
So any ideas on that, I guess, is the question.

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:18.360
AUDIENCE: So I'm just gonna answer the first
part of this, because I'm not sure if I have

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:20.100
an answer for the next two.

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:25.120
But something that I think is kind of hilarious
about like the integration of the internet

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:29.200
is that I think it's simultaneously making
us more literate and less literate.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:32.370
So like-- or like pressuring you to be more
literate.

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:34.880
So "pressuring" you to be more literate is
like, now there's a bunch of sources that

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:38.700
you can read, and like now you can be more
educated, it's all open to the public if you

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:40.580
have a computer.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:44.500
And then there's also like the pressure to
speak English properly, based off either,

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:49.600
you know, like there's one viral video, this
old grandma screaming at this Hispanic woman

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:52.690
in IHOP because she was paying in Spanish,
or something like that.

00:15:52.690 --> 00:15:57.029
So like it puts pressure on you to speak English
correctly, and then meanwhile, because of

00:15:57.029 --> 00:16:03.500
like things like Facebook and like any social
media, people like don't speak English correctly,

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:08.830
like typing, you know, abbreviations and stuff
that's acceptable in text, but like meanwhile

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:10.840
we're saying you should speak English properly.

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:14.480
So it's kind of a weird dichotomy.

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:20.360
GUEST SPEAKER: I'm interested in the age implications
of what you just said, kind of aligning--

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:26.970
kind of a critique towards the use of different
varieties of English with older generations

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:30.790
and the use of nonstandard varieties of English
with younger generations.

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:33.060
I'm interested in that.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:39.960
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I think-- I mean, I guess
like-- so like there's a grandma that I know

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:45.750
that's on Facebook, and like she makes sure
to type everything out, including punctuation.

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:49.779
And like her Facebook posts are longer and
they're more like here's a card, that type

00:16:49.779 --> 00:16:51.460
of thing.

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:56.360
Whereas like my Facebook, A, I'm just sharing
things, but B, if I write anything, it's probably

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:58.660
not good, proper English.

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:02.960
So I think it does depend on your age group,
but also like going back to the original point

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:07.120
of, you know, that grandma screaming at the
Hispanic woman in IHOP, like it's not just

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:10.470
like an older woman screaming because, you
know, she's more literate.

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:14.900
Like there's also like kids who think like,
oh, you have to speak English in here.

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:20.270
So like there is still that weirdness between
like how older people act on Facebook, and

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:21.270
stuff like that.

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:27.380
But I still think like the point of, you know,
pressuring people is regardless of age.

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:35.240
AUDIENCE: I think because we're gonna be talking
about language, which is dialect in this class,

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:39.740
I would kind of risk saying that we're kind
of creating a new language because of the

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:42.520
internet with like all the abbreviations and
things.

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:46.100
And there's the way-- the push for speed and
everything like that.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:50.440
So I think the reason that older generations
don't use it as much is because they're not

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:54.440
used to it, and they're in a different frame
of reference and they just haven't learned

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:57.380
this new dialect that we're starting to use.

00:17:57.380 --> 00:18:03.500
So I think that it's a good thing, in a sense,
but a lot of people who want that standard

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:07.400
American English are going to be pushing us
to go back to that, as opposed to using the

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:08.940
internet language.

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:14.860
But going as far as our changes in literacy
because of the internet, like I didn't know

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:16.340
that The Onion was satire, at first.

00:18:16.340 --> 00:18:18.590
Well, I just didn't know that The Onion existed.

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:20.173
And then when I saw that, I realized that
that was satire.

00:18:20.173 --> 00:18:25.050
I think that nuance like that is something
that you miss especially with the internet,

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:29.440
because you can't really express sarcasm very
clearly over just plain text.

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:35.040
And I think that's something that we're like
struggling with, because of the internet.

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:38.880
AUDIENCE: But I think that you said-- you
talked about The Onion.

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:45.060
I think that shows like Stephen Colbert or
the Colbert Report, and shows like that, show

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:49.200
that we have been dealing with that sort of
satire language issue.

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:54.000
Because for a long time, people [inaudible] the
Colbert Report didn't realize that it was satire.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:59.460
So I think that we've done this before, and
it's sort of a different cycle.

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:03.620
But now it's on the net instead of being TV.

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:06.100
AUDIENCE: I wanted to bounce off what you
originally said.

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:10.580
Like with like it's not a bad thing that we're
checking the language, I agree with this

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:13.400
It's kind of like [inaudible], as you mentioned.

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:17.660
Do you think like there are certain rules,
like the way we type, and like is there like

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:21.300
a way to be literate like with our new abbreviations?

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:23.380
AUDIENCE: I think there definitely is.

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:27.700
Like for instance, punctuating sentences with
like LOL or ha ha.

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:29.940
Like the beginning of the end, where you put
that.

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:31.660
Using emojis to get more emotion in it.

00:19:31.660 --> 00:19:36.080
Like there's definitely like a science to
it that we kind of fell into, but didn't realize

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:38.560
was [inaudible].

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:41.900
AUDIENCE: Yeah. OK, cool. This is interesting.

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:48.560
GUEST SPEAKER: My question is, so knowing
now that people grow up in the type of literacy,

00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:53.800
whether it's traditional or emancipatory,
what are the pros and cons of your type of

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:54.800
literacy?

00:19:54.800 --> 00:20:02.160
And explain why we should move ether towards
or away from an emancipatory literacy.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:15.360
AUDIENCE: I think you all gave some really
great points towards moving to emancipatory

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:16.860
literacy.

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:26.820
I mean, if it's helping people learn, I don't
see a down fall, especially because it's kind

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:28.940
of broadening their world view.

00:20:28.940 --> 00:20:34.980
A couple of presentations ago, there were
other students who were playing jeopardy with

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:39.420
AAE, and that was kind of like broadening
their worldview.

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:44.980
Like even if you went to the school and didn't
speak AAE you were learning about a new language

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:46.340
that you weren't familiar with.

00:20:46.340 --> 00:20:50.160
And so they were able to do this through the
emancipatory sort literacy.

00:20:50.160 --> 00:20:51.620
MICHEL DEGRAFF: What's AAE?

00:20:51.620 --> 00:21:00.800
AUDIENCE: Oh, African American English. 
Sorry. Not AAPE.

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:09.940
AUDIENCE: So kind of just playing devil's
advocate a little bit, I think-- I guess one

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:15.760
of the dangers of emancipatory literacy might
be kind of this non-standardization like with

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:17.880
issues like Common Core and things like that.

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:21.690
We kind of have an educational system that's
set up so that everybody's kind of put on

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:24.090
the same level, to some extent.

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:27.490
Like everybody has that same kind of baseline
education.

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:32.490
But like with emancipatory literacy, I think
you're going to find educational programs

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:37.409
are more geared towards a specific like cultural
norms and things of that nature related to

00:21:37.409 --> 00:21:39.160
whatever population it targets.

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:45.010
So I wonder if that could potentially produce
a system in which certain students, even though

00:21:45.010 --> 00:21:49.910
they're receiving an education that is geared
towards them, it's objectively not that same

00:21:49.910 --> 00:21:55.420
quality of student who are receiving more
of a traditional literacy approach.

00:21:55.420 --> 00:21:59.020
I'm just putting it out there.

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:04.220
GUEST SPEAKER: I want to push back on that
just a little bit, because I think that the

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:08.900
standardized approach to education is the
approach that's been taken with, you know,

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:11.090
teach for a test approach, and it's failed.

00:22:11.090 --> 00:22:15.200
It's been failing for a while now, and I think
that a lot of people in education policy and

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:17.600
even just teachers are recognizing that now.

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:22.750
And I think that education-- emancipatory
literacy gives us an interesting lens to think

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:25.580
about how to educate people so that they can
learn.

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:30.980
And I think maybe another place you can start
kind of investing energy is thinking of ways

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:35.929
in society that we can value those different
ways of knowing and being and learning, so

00:22:35.929 --> 00:22:40.430
that it's not just, you went to college, so
you deserve to be paid a lot of money kind

00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:43.890
of thing, and it's a, oh you can build a table
from scratch.

00:22:43.890 --> 00:22:47.820
How do you get paid to do that?

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:53.420
AUDIENCE: Where do you think MIT falls on
the spectrum of like emancipatory literacy?

00:22:53.420 --> 00:22:55.200
[laughing]

00:22:55.200 --> 00:22:57.540
GUEST SPEAKER: I think MIT is way far on the
tradition.

00:22:57.540 --> 00:23:01.040
MIT is the Alt right of traditional literacy,
I think.

00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:08.720
[laughing] I just think that there are-- I
think that the immense pressure that falls

00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:13.230
on students, just on students physical bodies,
is definitely a symptom of a larger problem.

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:19.510
And for me, the fact that that symptom is
not enough of an impetus to change, is heartbreaking.

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:24.700
Because I think that-- I didn't come to college
expecting it to be the best four years of

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:28.350
my life, but I didn't come here also expecting
to just suffer.

00:23:28.350 --> 00:23:34.809
[laughing] And so I think that there's definitely
got to be a way where I can bloom, can reach

00:23:34.809 --> 00:23:35.809
my best potential.

00:23:35.809 --> 00:23:36.920
And I love learning.

00:23:36.920 --> 00:23:44.370
I love-- like I love school, you know, and
the fact that I have just kept running up

00:23:44.370 --> 00:23:50.070
against the institution, I think, as a creative
person, has made me kind of step back and

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:54.940
think again about emancipatory literacy and
how I can do that after.

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:57.160
MICHEL DEGRAFF: So I have a question for you,
Cynthia.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:24:02.071
So given what you just said, how would you--
so if you were to be MIT's president, what

00:24:02.071 --> 00:24:03.280
would you change?

00:24:03.280 --> 00:24:07.830
What-- how would you make it better more emancipatory?

00:24:07.830 --> 00:24:12.780
Keeping in mind that there are certain goals
that you have to reach in terms of, say, chemical

00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:15.560
engineering, if you're a chemist, how would
you do that?

00:24:15.560 --> 00:24:18.780
How would you advise administration be better
on that score?

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:23.120
CYNTHIA: I think very first thing I would
do is have people who are more interested

00:24:23.120 --> 00:24:24.760
in research not be professors.

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:27.559
So the ones who are interested in research
should be doing research full time, and the

00:24:27.559 --> 00:24:29.660
people who are interested in teaching should
teach.

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:36.810
Because I think that that gives like-- that
gives people more impetus just to connect

00:24:36.810 --> 00:24:40.230
with their students and actually get to know
them, and I think in that kind of environment,

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:43.720
you would foster like a safer, healthier environment
for learning.

00:24:43.720 --> 00:24:49.010
Whereas if you have a professor who is more
interested in winning the Nobel Prize, like

00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:51.750
what are you-- what do you do coming up against
that?

00:24:51.750 --> 00:24:57.410
I also would have MIT think very, very hard
about where the funding is coming from?

00:24:57.410 --> 00:24:58.780
What it's going to?

00:24:58.780 --> 00:25:02.870
And how that kind of fits in the global system,
where MIT as an institution fits into the

00:25:02.870 --> 00:25:09.300
global system, as kind of not only an institution
that does propel you to a higher class, but

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:15.820
kind of does that at the expense of a lot
of other people and systems across the globe

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:17.370
even if it's not directly in the area.

00:25:17.370 --> 00:25:23.200
So I think that being more critical and intentional
about our values, and also maybe adding soul

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:27.060
to the end of that-- end of the that motto.

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:27.600
But--

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:28.780
MICHEL DEGRAFF: OK, OK.

00:25:28.780 --> 00:25:30.760
CYNTHIA: Yeah.

00:25:30.760 --> 00:25:34.020
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Thank you. I think we
should move to the third question.

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:35.060
Thank you.

00:25:35.060 --> 00:25:38.620
AUDIENCE: The last question is, from your
personal experiences, or what you've seen

00:25:38.630 --> 00:25:43.929
or read about, how do you think we can improve
students' experiences and confidence through

00:25:43.929 --> 00:25:46.030
intentional language use in schools?

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:49.960
Like what mottoes do you think are working,
or would work?

00:25:55.160 --> 00:25:56.380
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Go ahead Colin.

00:25:56.380 --> 00:26:02.540
AUDIENCE: So bouncing of that it seems we
get to [inaudible] thing where it [inaudible]

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:05.560
is inculcated in this standard curriculum.

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:09.660
It goes into society and then perpetuates
the standard curriculum back on the Indian

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:10.400
students.

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:16.380
So I'm curious about if anyone has thoughts
how you could make knowledge about-- because

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:21.800
one of the main hurdles is people instituting,
or interrogating the standard curriculum is

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:27.140
just strictly about how the story of America's
history has been with regarding the Native

00:26:27.140 --> 00:26:36.940
American genocide, slavery, and, for lack
of a better word, mean that it is manifest destiny.

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:43.360
How do you make people aware of that on a
level that they can see those things playing

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:46.560
out today, then work to interrogate that curriculum?

00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:50.000
Especially if they are coming from a background
where race isn't talked about in this family

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:56.140
because it's an uncomfortable topic, or if
they just haven't heard about these things

00:26:56.140 --> 00:26:59.220
necessarily because of school.

00:27:02.660 --> 00:27:06.740
AUDIENCE: For me, I feel like it's more about
where do you learn to interrogate your own

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:10.780
beliefs, and I feel like that's hard to find.

00:27:10.780 --> 00:27:14.799
And I came to-- this class was a huge part
of that for me.

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:19.180
And I don't know if that because I started
out, or if I just happened to stumble upon

00:27:19.180 --> 00:27:23.160
a place where I learned to interrogate my
own beliefs.

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:28.320
But I think this isn't a point to make for
themselves, but I don't really have an answer

00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:33.860
for how we get triple the value in evaluating
in interrogating your own beliefs, or how

00:27:33.860 --> 00:27:41.200
much more in [inaudible] learn to interrogate
your own beliefs before attacking someone else's.

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:45.600
AUDIENCE: Yeah, bouncing off of I think a
common theme that we come up in this class

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:51.220
is how do we have conversations with people
who don't want to these conversations?

00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:55.100
And I think the only way for them to figure
it out is for them to interrogate themselves,

00:27:55.100 --> 00:27:57.990
but most of those people won't.

00:27:57.990 --> 00:28:05.720
So finding ways to make them interrogate themselves,
I think is gonna be a common problem throughout

00:28:05.720 --> 00:28:06.600
history.

00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:10.940
And unfortunately I don't know I, personally,
have a great solution for that.

00:28:10.940 --> 00:28:15.810
But I think more opportunities like this--
I mean obviously you mentioned this before,

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:20.980
the people who are taking this class, were
not really diversified at all right?

00:28:20.980 --> 00:28:26.840
We all sought this class out mostly, so I
don't know how you can get someone who doesn't

00:28:26.840 --> 00:28:29.440
want interrogate themselves in to a situation
like this.

00:28:29.440 --> 00:28:35.280
So maybe mandating something like this where
you have to go a black history class, or a

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:41.840
Native American history class, or whatever
might force people to start talking about

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:42.840
it.

00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:46.020
The history will speak for itself.

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:49.940
I think that history is-- if you know the
full history of the United States for example--

00:28:49.940 --> 00:28:53.800
it's pretty terrible and I think that you
do need to know the whole history, and also

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:59.080
need to get the alternative sides and have
those be integrated into the curriculum.

00:28:59.080 --> 00:29:05.700
I also was interested in thinking about entry
points into these conversations, and ways

00:29:05.700 --> 00:29:08.440
that our humor can function as entry points
in these conversations.

00:29:08.440 --> 00:29:13.710
I've been having this conversation with a
friend of mine of how humor, for example,

00:29:13.710 --> 00:29:17.950
gives us a way to kind of express some of
these things that are more taboo, and kind

00:29:17.950 --> 00:29:22.220
of laugh them off, and kind of integrate them,
as opposed to everyone shunning them and people

00:29:22.220 --> 00:29:23.220
feeling bottled.

00:29:23.220 --> 00:29:30.299
So I am interested about humor and art specifically
as entry point into these conversations.

00:29:30.300 --> 00:29:33.340
MICHEL DEGRAFF: [inaudible].

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:40.059
So Dana, I think what you say in a way answers
the concern that Shope expressed right?

00:29:40.059 --> 00:29:43.180
Because Shope put it as dual choice.

00:29:43.180 --> 00:29:45.860
That you either do this or you do that, right?

00:29:45.860 --> 00:29:47.480
So Dana is right, right Shope,?

00:29:47.480 --> 00:29:53.600
One can imagine having in the class-- the
principle math class, chemistry class-- paying

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:55.000
attention to those issues.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:30:00.700
In fact there room for that, even if you take
a simple photography class one can even think

00:30:00.700 --> 00:30:04.900
about what kind of choices go into making
your camera?

00:30:04.900 --> 00:30:08.000
And actually, I have friends who are photographers
and they explain to me that actually most

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:11.300
cameras were not geared to take pictures of
black people.

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:14.860
So even as a white photographer he finds that
when he takes a picture with his regular Cannon

00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:19.260
of a white person it comes much better than
when takes it of a black person.

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:26.220
Why? Because the lenses are designed with a
normal gaze of the white subject right?

00:30:26.220 --> 00:30:30.040
So he had to tweak his camera to make it take
good pictures of black people.

00:30:30.040 --> 00:30:35.860
So even there, even in physics there's a way
to interrogate the curriculum.

00:30:35.860 --> 00:30:39.280
Why selections are made with the lens for
example?

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:44.420
So in a way it could both, you could both
have the necessary interest in human beings

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:47.760
the quote unquote more "traditional curriculum".

00:30:47.760 --> 00:30:51.920
And going back to the other point, can you
imagine also in history, in biology, biology

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:59.160
could include text like I mentioned earlier,
The Mismeasure of Man if you wish, goes into

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:01.680
lots of integral issues about biology.

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:05.660
About how we make-- heads were measured to
show that humans are as intelligent.

00:31:05.660 --> 00:31:10.340
At Harvard for example, for many years when
you were coming in you had to undress and

00:31:10.340 --> 00:31:15.320
they took your picture naked, and they would
at your picture to decide what kind of students

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:20.980
you would become based on physical traits,
like because your head.

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:22.720
This was Harvard.

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:27.820
I guess this was one proponent of what was
called then phrenology.

00:31:27.820 --> 00:31:29.580
Which is the measure of--

00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:33.980
So there is plenty, plenty of room to integrate
those kind intricacies within traditional

00:31:33.980 --> 00:31:34.980
curriculum.

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:37.280
OK, so thank you very much.

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:45.020
[applause]

